{
  "title": "LabOS Transport: Calibration, Feedback and Physics-Guided Planning",
  "section": "Transport experiment workflow",
  "note": "This is a transport workflow design, complementing the general LabOS architecture. The left column separates the server-to-worker HTTP(S) link, local driver APIs and physical instrument interfaces. A master lock-in supplies sine excitation through a series resistor and reads Vxx at the fundamental; a follower reads Vxy at a selected harmonic using a physical reference. Dedicated calibration stages reuse the configured readout paths; no extra simultaneous measurement channel or automatic rewiring is assumed. Autonomous calibration adjusts bounded software settings, repeats references and issues a versioned calibration record. Raw X/Y and reference phase are retained, rather than auto-phasing an unknown second-harmonic signal to zero. The central loop qualifies each completed point or chunk, proposes a revision and validates a new stage version before dispatch. Recoverable quality problems can return to calibration; faults or uncertain commands require pause and reconciliation. The server-local theory/compute coupling is explicitly planned. It receives versioned qualified-data snapshots, compares transport, thermal and circuit hypotheses and ranks informative candidate measurements within the operating envelope. This is not a claim of deployed transport autonomy or measured material results.",
  "source": "Original LabOS workflow design; supporting primary sources are linked in the bilingual companion document.",
